// THE execute-once canary. A compound assignment reads and writes the same
// place, so the place must be resolved ONCE — if the target and index
// expressions were evaluated separately for the read and for the write, every
// call inside them would run twice.
//
// `f()` and `g()` each print a distinct marker before returning an index, so
// double evaluation is directly visible as a duplicated marker rather than
// inferred. Expected output starts `7` then `8`, exactly once each.
//
// A stronger variant — where the second evaluation returns a DIFFERENT index and
// so writes a different element — is not expressible today: a function that
// mutates a top-level variable runs on the interpreter but does not lower on the
// JIT (exit 127), so a shared counter cannot be asserted across both backends.
// The duplicated marker is the cross-backend signal.
fnc: t64 f() {
    print(7)
    return 0
}
fnc: t64 g() {
    print(8)
    return 1
}
a: [2: [3: t64]] = [[10, 20, 30], [40, 50, 60]]
a:[f()]:[g()] += 5
print(a:[0]:[0])
print(a:[0]:[1])
print(a:[0]:[2])
print(a:[1]:[1])
